          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is there a case where two conditions documented together must NOT be linked by the coder?",
          "answer": "Yes. According to the coding guidance retrieved, there are cases where two conditions documented together should not be reported together.\n\n- **Excludes1** (from *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*): the two conditions cannot both be present, and the codes are never reported together. The retrieved text cuts off partway through this section, so I can't give the full wording.\n- **Excludes2** (same document): the excluded condition is not part of the code above it, and a person can have both, so both may be reported. The two notes look alike but mean opposite things about whether a second code is allowed.\n- **Asthma** (*Coding asthma: J45*): status asthmaticus and acute exacerbation are alternatives. When status asthmaticus is documented, the code does not also say acute exacerbation. The document says choosing between them is a documentation question, not a judgment the coder makes.\n\nThe guidance also warns against over-linking. The \"with\" presumption, where the index links two conditions, applies only to the pairings the index lists. The type 2 diabetes document (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11*) says a condition appearing on the same problem list is not automatically a complication.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not give a full list of every exclusion pair, and they do not cover how to handle a specific chart. For a particular record, the coding manual and a trained medical coder should be checked.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
